{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent negative bias: forecasts are systematically too low versus outcomes, especially in rising regimes. Apply an upward level shift (≈ +2 to +5 EUR) with the uplift increasing with horizon (small at days 1–3, larger by days 10–30).",
    "Trend/acceleration underestimation: when the last 1–2 weeks show an upswing, the model lags and fails to capture momentum. Add a momentum term proportional to recent slope (e.g., 10–20 day trend) to steepen the forecast path.",
    "Horizon-specific drift error: short-horizon errors are modest, but mid/long-horizon (≈ day 7–25) errors widen materially. Use a ramping correction that grows over time rather than a flat adjustment.",
    "Over-mean-reversion: the model repeatedly pulls back toward ~62–66 while realized prices trend into ~67–70. Reduce mean-reversion strength (or raise the implied mean) when the 30-day history is making higher highs/higher lows.",
    "Post-breakout underreaction: after a regime shift upward, forecasts stay anchored near pre-breakout levels. Detect breakouts (price above recent range/MA) and apply a step-up correction to the whole forward curve."
  ]
}
